======================== =
=: Your Implementation : =
======================== =


**Class Overviews:**

- **Game:**
  The entry point of the game. It sets up the main game window, initializes the card
  layout for switching between the instructions screen and the game screen, and manages
  the transition between different game states.

- **NathanDisplay:**
  Extends `JPanel` and handles the core game logic and rendering. It manages game objects,
  handles user input, updates game state, and renders all visual elements, including the
  player, blocks, enemies, and win conditions.

- **NathanPlayer:**
  Represents the player character. Handles player movement, collision detection with
  other sprites, and respawning at activated saveblocks upon death.

- **NathanBlock:**
  Represents static wall blocks within the maze. These blocks are impassable barriers
  that the player and enemies must navigate around.

- **NathanEnemy:**
  Represents enemy characters that move within the maze. Handles enemy movement
  patterns, collision detection, and direction changes upon hitting obstacles.

- **NathanDeathblock:**
  Represents hazardous areas that can kill the player upon contact. Some deathblocks
  are "trick" blocks that blink, adding an element of timing to avoid them.

- **NathanSaveblock:**
  Represents savepoints within the maze. When the player reaches a saveblock, it
  becomes activated, setting a respawn point in case of the player's death.

- **NathanWinblock:**
  Represents the goal area of the maze. Reaching the winblock triggers the end
  screen, displaying the player's statistics.

- **NathanInstructionsScreen:**
  Provides the game instructions and a button to start the game. It explains the
  objective, controls, and various game elements to the player.

- **NathanMazeGenerator:**
  Handles the generation of the maze layout using a randomized depth-first search
  algorithm. It populates the 2D maze array with walls and paths, ensuring a solvable
  maze structure.

- **NathanSprite:**
  The abstract base class for all game entities. Defines common properties such as
  position, size, color, and visibility, as well as methods for rendering and
  collision detection.

**Significant Stumbling Blocks:**

One of the main challenges encountered was implementing the dynamic behavior of
"trick" deathblocks that blink on and off. Ensuring that these deathblocks correctly
toggle their visibility based on timing required careful management of game state and
synchronization with the game loop. Also, handling collision detection
accurately between the player, enemies, and other blocks necessitated thorough
testing to prevent unexpected behaviors.

**Design Evaluation:**

The game's design shows a clear separation of concerns. Each class is
responsible for a specific aspect of the game, such as rendering, input handling,
or game logic. Private state is well-encapsulated within classes, preventing
unintended external modifications and promoting modularity. For cis1200, the
`NathanPlayer` class manages its own position and movement without exposing
these details directly to other classes.

If given the chance to refactor, one improvement I could do would be to enhance the collision
detection system to be more scalable and efficient, possibly by implementing spatial
partitioning techniques. Additionally, integrating more advanced design patterns,
such as the Observer pattern for game state changes, could further decouple
components and facilitate easier maintenance and feature expansion.

